    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Fundamental Rights available ONLY to Indian citizens: Articles 15, 16, 19, 29, and 30.",
        "Right to Property is now a legal right under Article 300A in Part XII of the Constitution.",
        "Dr. B.R. Ambedkar called Article 32 (Right to Constitutional Remedies) the 'very heart and soul of the Constitution'."
      ],
      "id": "8w2vdyk"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Originally, the Constitution provided for seven Fundamental Rights, but currently there are only six.",
        "The Right to Property (Article 31) was removed from the list of Fundamental Rights by the 44th Amendment Act, 1978.",
        "Fundamental Rights are categorized into Right to Equality, Right to Freedom, Right against Exploitation, Right to Freedom of Religion, Cultural & Educational Rights, and Right to Constitutional Remedies.",
        "Part III of the Constitution is rightly described as the 'Magna Carta of India'.",
        "Fundamental Rights are justiciable, allowing individuals to move the courts directly for their enforcement."
      ],
      "id": "qccy28q"
    }
